t_chunk_s": 42730.7533731, "t_first_token_s": 42730.7533731, "t_start_s": 42730.6832334, "text": "A GPU (Graphics Processing Unit) outperforms a CPU (Central Processing Unit) at matrix multiplication due to its specialized architecture and parallel processing capabilities. Unlike CPUs, which execute instructions sequentially, GPUs are designed to handle massive parallel computations, making them ideal for matrix multiplication. Each GPU core can perform multiple matrix multiplications simultaneously, leveraging hundreds or thousands of cores to accelerate the computation. This allows GPUs to process large matrices much faster than CPUs, which are limited by their single-core design and sequential execution model. As a result, GPUs can achieve significant performance gains in matrix multiplication, making them a popular choice for applications such as scientific simulations, machine learning, and data analytics.", "total_duration_ns": 1092719500, "ttft_s": 0.07013970000116387, "valid": true}
